  "image_name": "13199f0c945555806db5c6e72fefb791.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a hierarchy of electron–hole complexes in semiconductors, likely in a 2D material, using simple band diagrams. It begins with optical excitation across the band gap: a photon promotes an electron from the valence band to the conduction band. The excited carriers can then bind into different many-body states, highlighted by dashed red ovals and their binding energies.\n\nThe simplest bound state is an exciton, made of one electron and one hole. With n-doping, an extra electron can join to form a trion, and two electron–hole pairs can bind as a biexciton. More complex charged states also appear, including a quinton with three electrons and two holes. Another panel shows a polaronic trion, where the trion is coupled to “soft phonons,” indicating interaction with lattice vibrations.\n\nThe final part extends the picture to a bilayer system. There, an interlayer exciton forms when the electron and hole reside in different layers, separated by a band offset between Layer 1 and Layer 2. Overall, the figure compares how different carrier numbers, doping, phonons, and layer structure produce a family of bound excitonic quasiparticles with distinct energy splittings.",
